What is JDBC?

JDBC (Java Database Connectivity) is an API (set of classes & interfaces) 
that allows Java programs to connect to a database, 
execute SQL queries, and retrieve results.

It’s part of the java.sql package.

JDBC Architecture:
Java Application
      ↓
 JDBC API (java.sql)
      ↓
 JDBC Driver
      ↓
 Database (MySQL, Oracle, PostgreSQL)

 
 Steps to connect to Database:
 | Step | Description          | Example                                                          |
| ---- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |
| 1️⃣  | Load the JDBC Driver | `Class.forName("com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver");`                     |
| 2️⃣  | Establish Connection | `Connection con = DriverManager.getConnection(url, user, pass);` |
| 3️⃣  | Create Statement     | `Statement stmt = con.createStatement();`                        |
| 4️⃣  | Execute SQL Query    | `ResultSet rs = stmt.executeQuery("SELECT * FROM emp");`         |
| 5️⃣  | Process Results      | `while(rs.next()) { ... }`                                       |
| 6️⃣  | Close Connection     | `con.close();`                                                   |
 
 
 | Interface / Class   | Purpose                                      |
| ------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |
| `DriverManager`     | Manages database drivers & connections       |
| `Connection`        | Represents connection to database            |
| `Statement`         | Executes SQL queries                         |
| `PreparedStatement` | Executes parameterized SQL queries (safer)   |
| `ResultSet`         | Represents data returned from a SELECT query |
| `SQLException`      | Handles database-related exceptions          |
 
 

| Type                | Description                           | Use Case                                   |
| ------------------- | ------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |
| `Statement`         | Executes static SQL queries           | For fixed SQL like `"SELECT * FROM table"` |
| `PreparedStatement` | Precompiled SQL with parameters (`?`) | For dynamic SQL; prevents SQL injection    |
| `CallableStatement` | Used to call stored procedures        | For DB functions/procedures                |


| Method            | Used With              | Purpose                             |
| ----------------- | ---------------------- | ----------------------------------- |
| `executeQuery()`  | SELECT                 | Returns `ResultSet`                 |
| `executeUpdate()` | INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE | Returns number of rows affected     |
| `execute()`       | Any SQL                | Returns boolean (true if ResultSet) |
 
 
 
 JDBC Exception Handling

All database errors throw SQLException.
You should handle them like this:
catch (SQLException e) {
    System.out.println("Error Code: " + e.getErrorCode());
    System.out.println("SQL State: " + e.getSQLState());
    System.out.println("Message: " + e.getMessage());
}
